    {
      "kind": "navigation",
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-measurable-probability-C025",
      "path": "handbook/parts/p15/measurable-probability.tex",
      "line_start": 462,
      "line_end": 470,
      "observation": "The boundary says the converse (every admissible nondecreasing right-continuous function is a distribution function) needs a measure-existence argument and points only to Durrett (thm. 1.2.2 p. 11; thms. 1.1.4, 1.1.9; app. A.1). It does not point to the handbook's own construction in the next chapter (handbook/parts/p15/measure-extension.tex, sec:p15-distribution-existence lines 267-319, thm:p15-cdf-existence lines 287-319), although it gives \\Cref pointers for the later integration, Radon-Nikodym and conditioning chapters. The boundary is accurate about this chapter. The missing pointer is a routing defect, not a coverage gap."
    },
    {
      "kind": "notation",
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-measurable-probability-C025",
      "path": "handbook/parts/p15/measurable-probability.tex",
      "line_start": 478,
      "line_end": 478,
      "observation": "'the point mass \\(P(X=a)\\)' uses an undecorated P. Everywhere else in the chapter the probability measure is \\mathbb P, and the point mass is written p_X(a)."
    },
    {
      "kind": "elliptical-step",
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-measurable-probability-C003",
      "path": "handbook/parts/p15/measurable-probability.tex",
      "line_start": 61,
      "line_end": 63,
      "observation": "The check that the finite-cofinite collection is an algebra covers complements and two-set unions. It does not state the third condition of the algebra definition at line 36: N+ belongs to A because its complement, the empty set, is finite. Extending two-set unions to finite unions is also left implicit. Each missing step follows at once from the stated facts, so the verdict is not changed."
    },
    {
      "kind": "elliptical-step",
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-measurable-probability-C007",
      "path": "handbook/parts/p15/measurable-probability.tex",
      "line_start": 166,
      "line_end": 171,
      "observation": "P(A_m)=2^{1-m} is displayed without its one-line computation, which is the complement of {1,...,m-1} combined with the finite geometric identity at lines 159-161. The verdict is not changed."
    },
    {
      "kind": "elliptical-step",
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-measurable-probability-C008",
      "path": "handbook/parts/p15/measurable-probability.tex",
      "line_start": 196,
      "line_end": 199,
      "observation": "Finite additivity of the 0/1 assignment states two of its three cases. The finite-plus-finite case is left to the earlier remark at line 62. No reason is written for why two disjoint subsets of N+ cannot both be cofinite: one lies inside the finite complement of the other. Nor is it written that no subset of N+ is both finite and cofinite, which the assignment needs to be well defined. Each step follows at once, so the verdict is not changed."
    },
    {
      "kind": "source-variant-note",
      "component_id": "ED26-P15-CH-measurable-probability-C016",
      "path": "handbook/parts/p15/measurable-probability.tex",
      "line_start": 302,
      "line_end": 321,
      "observation": "The held source differs on three points, at Durrett pp. 16-17 (PDF pp. 24-25). (1) Almost-sure convergence is defined as P(lim X_n exists)=1, with Omega_o={limsup X_n - liminf X_n = 0}. (2) Extended random variables may be defined on a domain D in F. (3) The sigma-field on the extended line is the Borel field of the order topology. The handbook instead defines almost-sure convergence only to a specified real limit, defines extended variables on all of Omega, and generates the sigma-field from the closed rays [-inf,a]. Lines 350-352 address the infinite-subtraction problem, but none of these differences is marked as a departure from the source. This audit may not open the scope record that lists the identified source variants, so the note is not graded. The source version itself ('author version 5 dated 11 January 2019', body lines 12-14) matches the held PDF's title page."
    },
